  <p class="eyebrow">Part A:</p>
  <h1 class="display sm">A Gentle<br>Introduction<br>to Stoicism (1/2)</h1>

  <div class="cols c3" style="margin-top:32px">
    <div class="colblock">
      <h3 class="kicker">What Actually Is Stoicism?</h3>
      <p class="body">Let's start with what Stoicism <em>isn't</em> (because honestly, the internet has done this philosophy dirty).</p>
      <p class="body">Stoicism is not about becoming an emotionless robot who never cries. It's not about suppressing your feelings or pretending everything is fine when it's clearly not. And it's definitely not about being "stoic" in the way people usually use that word today (you know, being emotionless to the point of being blank).</p>
      <p class="body"><strong>Real Stoicism</strong> is about not letting your emotions control you. It's a full-on life philosophy rooted in <em>four core virtues</em> (more on those later); it teaches you to <em>respond instead of react</em>, to <em>focus on what you can control</em>, and to <em>live with intention</em> no matter what life throws your way. It teaches you how to navigate life's inevitable ups and downs with grace, clarity, and inner strength, while still being fully, beautifully human.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="colblock">
      <h3 class="kicker">A Tiny Bit of Its Ancient Backstory</h3>
      <p class="body">Stoicism originated in <em>Athens, Greece</em>, around 300 BCE, founded by Zeno of Citium, a merchant-turned-philosopher from Cyprus. He began teaching in a public colonnade called the Stoa Poikile (Painted Porch); which is where the philosophy gets its name!</p>
      <p class="body">And while Stoicism evolved over time, it was the <em>Roman era</em> that really brought it to life through a few standout voices (the ones whose ideas still feel surprisingly relevant today!).</p>
      <p class="body">The three philosophers you'll hear about most are:</p>
    </div>

    <div class="colblock">
      <div class="phils">
        <div class="phil">
          <span class="frame">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 512 512" preserveAspectRatio="xMidYMid meet"><path fill="#D64C63" d="M256 37.4c-28.1 0-50.9 21.3-50.9 59.9 0 29.8 12.9 58.3 12.9 58.3l15-18.5h12.6v-22.7H218V93.5h76v20.9h-27.6v22.7H279l15 18.5s12.9-28.5 12.9-58.3c0-38.6-22.8-59.9-50.9-59.9zm-66.9 72.5c-1.3 8.7-1.9 17.8-1.9 27.2 0 64.2 30.8 106.4 68.8 106.4s68.8-42.2 68.8-106.4c0-9.4-.6-18.5-1.9-27.2-2.8 28.3-13.7 52.6-13.7 52.6L298.1 187l-27-33.2h-30.2l-27 33.2-11.1-24.5s-10.9-24.3-13.7-52.6zm58.6 53.7h16.6v12.7h-16.6v-12.7zm71 19.7v.2-.2zm-145.5 5-36.9 9.3L168 339.4h61.8l24-75.1c-34.7-1.2-66.9-28.9-80.6-76zm165.6 0c-10.5 36.2-32 61-57.2 71L256 339.4h21.7l20.7-70.4 12 3.5-19.6 66.9h16.9l36.4-125.8 12 3.5-35.4 122.3H344l31.7-141.8-36.9-9.3zM197 360.6v94h18v-64h82v64h18v-94H197zm36 48v46h46v-46h-46zm-69.3 64-14 18h212.6l-14-18H163.7z"/></svg>
          </span>
          <div><h4>Marcus Aurelius</h4><p>Basically an emperor who kept a philosophical journal (his "Meditations") where he pretty much reflected on life and talked himself through anxiety, imposter syndrome, and the stress of running an empire. Pretty iconic tbh.</p></div>
        </div>
        <div class="phil">
          <span class="frame">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 512 512" preserveAspectRatio="xMidYMid meet"><path fill="#D64C63" d="M256 37.4c-28.1 0-50.9 21.3-50.9 59.9 0 29.8 12.9 58.3 12.9 58.3l15-18.5h12.6v-22.7H218V93.5h76v20.9h-27.6v22.7H279l15 18.5s12.9-28.5 12.9-58.3c0-38.6-22.8-59.9-50.9-59.9zm-66.9 72.5c-1.3 8.7-1.9 17.8-1.9 27.2 0 64.2 30.8 106.4 68.8 106.4s68.8-42.2 68.8-106.4c0-9.4-.6-18.5-1.9-27.2-2.8 28.3-13.7 52.6-13.7 52.6L298.1 187l-27-33.2h-30.2l-27 33.2-11.1-24.5s-10.9-24.3-13.7-52.6zm58.6 53.7h16.6v12.7h-16.6v-12.7zm71 19.7v.2-.2zm-145.5 5-36.9 9.3L168 339.4h61.8l24-75.1c-34.7-1.2-66.9-28.9-80.6-76zm165.6 0c-10.5 36.2-32 61-57.2 71L256 339.4h21.7l20.7-70.4 12 3.5-19.6 66.9h16.9l36.4-125.8 12 3.5-35.4 122.3H344l31.7-141.8-36.9-9.3zM197 360.6v94h18v-64h82v64h18v-94H197zm36 48v46h46v-46h-46zm-69.3 64-14 18h212.6l-14-18H163.7z"/></svg>
          </span>
          <div><h4>Epictetus</h4><p>Started as a slave, became a teacher, and developed some of the most practical wisdom about what we can and can't control. His teachings are essentially ancient CBT (Cognitive Behavioral Therapy).</p></div>
        </div>
