<!-- ============ PART A 2/2 ============ -->
<section class="page" id="p-a2">
  <div class="runhead">
    <span>01–The Foundation</span>
    <span class="tag">/edu/</span>
    <div class="dots"><i class="on"></i><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i></div>
  </div>

  <p class="eyebrow">Part A:</p>
  <h1 class="display sm">A Gentle<br>Introduction<br>to Stoicism (2/2)</h1>

  <div class="cols c3" style="margin-top:32px">
    <div class="colblock">
      <h3 class="kicker">The Core Idea That Changes Everything</h3>
      <p class="body">Here's the central insight that makes Stoicism so powerful:</p>
      <p class="body"><strong>You can't control what happens to you, but you can always control how you respond.</strong></p>
      <p class="body">Notice how this isn't toxic positivity telling you to "just think positive." It rather is the recognition that your peace, your strength, and your sense of self don't have to be at the mercy of external circumstances, other people's opinions, or even your own first emotional reactions.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="colblock">
      <h3 class="kicker">The Four Pillars of Stoic Teachings</h3>
      <p class="body">Stoics were also big on moral principles. Here's an overview over the four core stoic virtues I mentioned earlier:</p>
      <ol class="pillars">
        <li><b>Wisdom (Prudence):</b><span>Seeing situations clearly, understanding what you can and can't control, and making decisions from clarity rather than reactivity.</span></li>
        <li><b>Courage (Bravery):</b><span>Not just physical bravery, but the everyday courage to be yourself, have difficult conversations, and do what's right even when it's hard.</span></li>
        <li><b>Justice (Fairness):</b><span>Treating yourself and others fairly, which includes setting healthy boundaries and standing up for what matters.</span></li>
        <li><b>Temperance (Self-Control):</b><span>Finding balance and moderation, whether that's with food, emotions, work, or relationships.</span></li>
      </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="colblock">
      <h3 class="kicker">Why This Ancient Stuff Matters Right Now</h3>
      <p class="body">The thing is, our brains are essentially the same as they were 2000 years ago, but our environment has gotten infinitely more complex.</p>
      <p class="body">We're dealing with information overload, social media comparison, decision fatigue, and a pace of life that would make Marcus Aurelius need a nap for suuure.</p>
      <p class="body">What I love about Stoicism is that it gives us tools that have been field-tested across centuries of human experience. It doesn't live in the clouds; it lives in your <em>daily decisions</em>. Unlike abstract philosophies that stay theoretical, or some forms of therapy that rely on digging endlessly into the past, Stoicism is <em>practical</em>. It meets you <em>right now</em> and asks:</p>
      <p class="body"><em>What's in your control? How do you want to show up today?</em></p>
      <p class="body">While other systems might explain the <em>why</em>, Stoicism shines in the <em>how</em>. That's what makes it timeless (and kind of badass tbh).</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="runfoot">
    <span class="motto">Your mind is your most intimate space.</span>
    <span class="pg">9</span>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ PART B 1/3 ============ -->
<section class="page" id="p-b1">
  <div class="runhead">
    <span>01–The Foundation</span>
    <span class="tag">/edu/</span>
    <div class="dots"><i class="on"></i><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i></div>
  </div>

  <p class="eyebrow">Part B:</p>
  <h1 class="display sm">Understanding<br>Your Emotional<br>Operating<br>System (1/3)</h1>

  <div class="cols c3" style="margin-top:30px">
    <div class="colblock">
      <h3 class="kicker">Your Mind: The Most Intimate Space You'll Ever Live In</h3>
      <p class="body">Think about it: you're going to spend every single day for the rest of your life inside your own head. Shouldn't you get to know the neighborhood a little better?</p>
      <p class="body">Most of us are running on emotional autopilot, reacting to thoughts and feelings as if they're absolute truth rather than just mental weather passing through.</p>
      <p class="body">But here's the thing: <strong>awareness changes everything.</strong></p>
    </div>

    <div class="colblock">
      <h3 class="kicker">The CBT Connection: How Your Mind Actually Works</h3>
      <p class="body">Cognitive Behavioral Therapy (which is kinda like modern Stoicism but with research backing) shows us that thoughts, feelings, and behaviors are all interconnected in a triangle:</p>
      <svg class="triangle-mini" viewBox="0 0 210 134">
        <defs><marker id="ahm" markerWidth="9" markerHeight="9" refX="5" refY="3" orient="auto"><path d="M0 0 L6 3 L0 6 Z" fill="#D64C63"/></marker></defs>
        <text x="105" y="20" text-anchor="middle">Thoughts</text>
        <text x="30" y="128" text-anchor="start">Behaviors</text>
        <text x="180" y="128" text-anchor="end">Feelings</text>
        <g stroke="#D64C63" stroke-width="2" fill="none" stroke-linecap="round">
          <line x1="62" y1="100" x2="100" y2="52" marker-end="url(#ahm)"/>
          <line x1="148" y1="100" x2="110" y2="52" marker-end="url(#ahm)"/>
          <line x1="64" y1="112" x2="146" y2="112" marker-start="url(#ahm)" marker-end="url(#ahm)"/>
        </g>
      </svg>
    </div>

    <div class="colblock">
      <p class="body">When you change one point of the triangle, the other two shift as well. Here's how it might play out:</p>
      <p class="body">Let's say your friend doesn't text you back for days.</p>
      <ul class="tick">
        <li><em>Thought:</em> "She's mad at me" or "I said something wrong"</li>
        <li><em>Feeling:</em> Anxiety, worry, maybe some shame</li>
        <li><em>Behavior:</em> Send three follow-up texts or spiral into overthinking</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="body">Your behavior then very likely leads to more thoughts and feelings that just aren't helping the situation.</p>
      <p class="body">But what if we interrupted that triangle earlier? What if instead of immediately believing that first thought, we paused and asked: <em>"Is this actually true, or is this just my brain trying to protect me from uncertainty?"</em></p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="runfoot">
    <span class="motto">Your mind is your most intimate space.</span>
    <span class="pg">10</span>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ PART B 2/3 ============ -->
<section class="page" id="p-b2">
  <div class="runhead">
    <span>01–The Foundation</span>
    <span class="tag">/edu/</span>
    <div class="dots"><i class="on"></i><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i></div>
  </div>

  <p class="eyebrow">Part B:</p>
  <h1 class="display sm">Understanding<br>Your Emotional<br>Operating<br>System (2/3)</h1>

  <div class="cols c3" style="margin-top:30px">
    <div class="colblock">
      <h3 class="kicker">Your Brain's Security System</h3>
      <p class="body">Your brain has one primary job: keep you alive. It's constantly scanning for threats, which was super helpful when we were dodging saber-tooth tigers, but less helpful when it treats a delayed text response like a life-or-death situation.</p>
      <p class="body">This is why your mind might:</p>
      <ul class="tick">
        <li>Jump to worst-case scenarios</li>
        <li>Replay conversations looking for problems</li>
        <li>Create stories about what other people are thinking</li>
        <li>Catastrophize small setbacks</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="body">Your brain isn't broken, it's just doing its job a little too enthusiastically (much like an overprotective big brother).</p>
    </div>

    <div class="colblock">
      <h3 class="kicker">The Space Between Stimulus and Response</h3>
      <p class="body">Here's where it gets really practical. Viktor Frankl (a Holocaust survivor and psychiatrist) said:</p>
      <blockquote class="frankl">"Between stimulus and response there is a space. In that space is our power to choose our response. In our response lies our growth and our freedom."</blockquote>
      <p class="body">That space is where the magic happens. That space is where you get to decide who you want to be in any given moment.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="colblock">
      <h3 class="kicker">Thoughts Are Not Facts (Really, They're Not)</h3>
      <p class="body">Just because you think something doesn't make it true.</p>
      <p class="body">Thoughts are more like suggestions your brain offers up. Some are helpful, some not so much.</p>
      <p class="body">The Stoic practice (similar to the traditional meditation practice btw) is <em>learning to observe your thoughts with curiosity</em> rather than immediately believing them or being swept away by them. It's like being a scientist or a curious child studying your own mind.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="runfoot">
    <span class="motto">Your mind is your most intimate space.</span>
    <span class="pg">11</span>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ PART B 3/3 ============ -->
<section class="page" id="p-b3">
  <div class="runhead">
    <span>01–The Foundation</span>
    <span class="tag">/edu/</span>
    <div class="dots"><i class="on"></i><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i></div>
  </div>

  <p class="eyebrow">Part B:</p>
  <h1 class="display sm">Understanding<br>Your Emotional<br>Operating<br>System (3/3)</h1>

  <div class="cols c3" style="margin-top:30px">
    <div class="colblock">
      <h3 class="kicker">Why Sensitivity Is Actually Your Superpower</h3>
      <p class="body">If you're someone who feels everything deeply, you might think Stoicism isn't for you. But here's the plot twist: sensitive people often make the best Stoics because you most likely already have emotional intelligence! You just need to learn how to channel it.</p>
      <p class="body">Your ability to feel deeply means you:</p>
      <ul class="tick">
        <li>Pick up on subtleties others miss</li>
        <li>Have natural empathy and compassion</li>
        <li>Can access the full range of human experience</li>
        <li>Are motivated by meaning, not just surface-level goals</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="body">Remember that the goal isn't to <em>feel less</em>; it's to <em>feel wisely</em>.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="colblock">
      <h3 class="kicker">The Neuroscience Bit</h3>
      <p class="cap" style="display:block;margin-bottom:8px">(Don't worry, we'll keep it simple)</p>
      <p class="body">Your brain has two main operating systems:</p>
      <div class="twosys">
        <div class="sys"><b>1. The reactive system (amygdala/limbic system)</b><span>Fast, emotional, designed for survival.</span></div>
        <div class="sys"><b>2. The reflective system (prefrontal cortex)</b><span>Slower, rational, designed for complex thinking.</span></div>
      </div>
      <p class="body">Most of our suffering comes from letting the reactive system drive the bus. Instead, we want it to be in the passenger seat: keeping us company on the journey, but not letting it choose our destination, decide what route we're taking to get there, or have control over how fast we're going.</p>
      <p class="body">Making those decisions ultimately is the driver's job, which should be our prefrontal cortex (I mean, it's there for a reason…).</p>
      <p class="body">Stoic practices help strengthen your prefrontal cortex, giving you more access to that wise, reflective part of yourself.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="colblock">
      <div style="display:flex;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px">
        <div style="flex:1;min-width:96px">
