<!-- ============ EX 1.3 DISTORTIONS TABLE (1/2) ============ -->
<section class="page" id="p-ex3">
  <div class="runhead">
    <span>01–The Foundation</span>
    <span class="tag">/ex 1.3/</span>
    <div class="dots"><i class="on"></i><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i></div>
  </div>

  <div class="exhead">
    <div style="flex:1;min-width:280px">
      <h1 class="display sm">Cognitive<br>Distortions<br>Bingo (1/2)</h1>
    </div>
    <div class="purpose-bar">
      <div><b>Purpose:</b> Recognize common thinking patterns that create unnecessary emotional drama and learn to reframe them with more balanced perspectives.</div>
      <div><b>Time estimate:</b> 10–15 minutes</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <hr class="rule" style="margin:24px 0 28px">

  <div class="grid2" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2.55fr;gap:22px 46px;align-items:start">
    <div>
      <p class="body">For this exercise, we need a bit more background knowledge, so here we go:</p>
      <p class="body">One of the most powerful tools from practical psychology is learning to spot common thinking errors called <em>cognitive distortions</em>; mental shortcuts our brains take that twist reality in unhelpful ways.</p>
      <p class="body">You can think of cognitive distortions as fun-house mirrors: They reflect something real, but the image is warped in predictable ways.</p>
      <p class="body">Once you know what to look for, you can start recognizing when your brain is showing you a distorted picture.</p>
      <p class="body">Here are some of the most common cognitive distortions that fuel overthinking:</p>

      <div class="callout" style="margin-top:16px">
        <div class="ct"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5"><circle cx="12" cy="7" r="3"/><circle cx="7" cy="15" r="3"/><circle cx="17" cy="15" r="3"/></svg>Quick reference</div>
        <p>Find a quick reference version of this table in the appendix A2 (p. 130) for when you need a fast brain-drama decoder.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <table class="dist">
      <thead><tr><th>Thinking Error</th><th>Description</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td class="name">1. All-or-Nothing Thinking</td><td>Seeing things in black-and-white categories with no in-between.</td><td class="ex">"If I don't get an A on this project, my entire semester is ruined."</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="name">2. Overgeneralization</td><td>Making broad conclusions based on a single incident.</td><td class="ex">"That guy ghosted me after our date. No one ever wants a second date with me."</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="name">3. Mental Filtering</td><td>Focusing only on the negative and ignoring the positive.</td><td class="ex">"I got 9 compliments and one critique, but clearly I'm not good enough."</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="name">4. Discounting the Positive</td><td>Rejecting or minimizing good things as if they don't count.</td><td class="ex">"My friend said my artwork is beautiful, but she's just saying that because she feels sorry for me."</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="name">5. Jumping to Conclusions</td><td>Making assumptions without solid evidence. Includes Mind Reading and Fortune Telling.</td><td class="ex">Mind Reading: "They think I'm boring." Fortune Telling: "I'm going to fail this interview."</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="name">6. Catastrophizing</td><td>Expecting the worst or blowing things out of proportion.</td><td class="ex">"If I stumble during my speech at this wedding, everyone will remember it forever and the bride will hate me."</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="name">7. Emotional Reasoning</td><td>Believing that how you feel must be true.</td><td class="ex">"I feel uncomfortable at this party, so everyone must be judging me and thinking I don't belong here."</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="name">8. Should Statements</td><td>Using "should," "must," or "ought to" in rigid, self-critical ways.</td><td class="ex">"I should be able to finish this entire to-do list today. Anything less means I'm lazy and undisciplined."</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="name">9. Labeling &amp; Mislabeling</td><td>Assigning harsh global labels to yourself or others.</td><td class="ex">"I forgot my friend's birthday. I'm such a horrible, selfish person."</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="name">10. Personalization</td><td>Thinking things that have nothing to do with you are actually about you.</td><td class="ex">"She's upset — I must've done something wrong."</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="name">11. Blaming</td><td>Placing all responsibility on others or external factors.</td><td class="ex">"My life is a mess because of my parents."</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="name">12. Magnification &amp; Minimization</td><td>Exaggerating flaws and down-playing strengths.</td><td class="ex">"Sure, I organized a successful fundraiser, but anyone could have done that. Meanwhile, the accounting error I made proves that I'm incompetent."</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="name">13. Control Fallacies</td><td>Believing you either have total control or none at all.</td><td class="ex">"If I can't make my partner happy all the time, then I'm completely useless in this relationship."</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="name">14. Owning the Truth</td><td>Believing your perspective is the only correct one and dismissing other viewpoints.</td><td class="ex">"I know for a fact that my way of organizing this project is right, and everyone who disagrees just doesn't understand how things work."</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="name">15. Entitlement</td><td>Expecting rewards or outcomes simply because you believe you deserve them.</td><td class="ex">"I've worked hard, so I should be promoted."</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
